title: Display settings
intro: An options menu picks the window's size, VSync and a frame cap with no code, and the game remembers them.
project: Arcade | Display Options

chapter: A window size | Choice > Setting = Window Size
scene: Options
say: The Arcade template's options menu has a choice for the difficulty. Any choice can pick one of the game's own settings instead.
select: Difficulty
rename: Window Size
set: Choice.Setting = WindowSize
set: Choice.Format = < {0} >
explain: field:Choice.Setting | Setting | Set to Window Size, it offers the common 16:9 window sizes that fit the player's screen in place of its own options, and picking one resizes the built game's window there and then.
expectvalue: Window Size.Choice.Setting = WindowSize
say: Fullscreen stays borderless at the screen's own resolution: Griff never changes the display's mode. The window opens at the size picked next time.

chapter: VSync and a frame cap | Toggle > VSync, Choice > Frame Cap
select: Sound
rename: VSync
set: Toggle.Setting = VSync
set: Toggle.OnText = VSync: On
set: Toggle.OffText = VSync: Off
explain: field:Toggle.Setting | VSync | A toggle set to VSync keeps frames in step with the screen while it's on, as Griff always has. Off, the game draws each frame as soon as the last is done.
expectvalue: VSync.Toggle.Setting = VSync
deselect
create: Choice
rename: Frame Cap
set: Transform.Position = 5.5, 1.4, 0
set: Choice.Setting = FrameCap
set: Choice.Format = Cap: < {0} >
explain: field:Choice.Setting | Frame cap | A choice set to Frame Cap offers Off, 30, 60, 120, 144, 165 and 240 frames a second. With VSync on, a cap of 30 on a 60 hertz screen draws every other frame.
expectvalue: Frame Cap.Choice.Setting = FrameCap
say: The cap only changes how often the picture is drawn. The game's steps stay at its step rate, so a capped game plays at the same speed.

chapter: Pick them | Play
play
wait: 1
gameclick: Window Size
wait: 0.5
expectvalue: Window Size.Choice.Value = 1366x768
say: Picked in the editor, the size is kept for the built game: there's no window of its own to resize here.
gameclick: Frame Cap
wait: 0.5
expectvalue: Frame Cap.Choice.Value = 30
gameclick: VSync
wait: 0.5
expectvalue: VSync.Toggle.IsOn = false
say: The editor's Game tab keeps in step with the screen whatever VSync says, since the editor shares the game's thread, but the cap counts here too. All of them are remembered next time, and scripts have them as Game dot Window Size, VSync and Frame Cap. A slider set to UI Scale makes the HUD bigger or smaller the same way.
stop
